  abstract = {Examined whether speakers use gestures differently when those gestures have the potential to communicate and when they do not in 16 undergraduate students. Ss watched an animated cartoon and narrated the cartoon story to a listener in 2 parts: one part in normal face-to-face interaction and one part with visibility between speaker and listener blocked by a screen. The session was videotaped with a hidden camera. Gestures were identified and classified into 2 categories: representational gestures, which are gestures that depict semantic content related to speech by virtue of handshape, placement, or motion, and beat gestures, which are simple, rhythmic gestures that do not convey semantic content. Results indicate that speakers produced representational gestures at a higher rate in the face-to-face condition; however, they continued to produce some representational gestures in the screen condition, when their listeners could not see the gestures. Speakers produced beat gestures at comparable rates under both conditions. Descriptions of cartoon episodes are appended. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)},
