  abstract = {The following study examines the relationship between discourse markers, or vocal hiccups such as um, uh, like and you know, and speaker use of such markers in extemporaneous and impromptu speaking. One hundred and fifty speeches were transcribed and the number of markers used by each speaker was calculated. A gender comparison reveals no significant difference between speaker usage of two of the markers (um, uh). The study does however show a significant gender difference in the usage of the other two markers (like, you know). Finally, this study draws conclusions about discourse marker usage, speaker credibility and competitive success.},
